// MGMT question bank. Written from the LMS Module 1-5 Ebooks and Study Guides only.
// Row: [module, question, options, correctIndex (0-based), reason, source, wrong-option notes]
window.QBANK = {
  subject: "Principles of Management", code: "MGMT",
  modules: {"1":"Introduction to Management","2":"Evolution of Management Thought","3":"Social & Ethical Responsibilities of Management","4":"Span of Control","5":"Managerial Decision Making"},
  rows: [
[1,"Which author defined management as 'the art of getting things done through and with people in formally organised groups'?",["Henry Fayol","Harold Koontz","F.W. Taylor","Peter Drucker"],1,"Harold Koontz specifically defined management as the art of getting things done through and with people in formally organised groups.","Ebook","Other options: Fayol's definition includes 'forecast, plan, organise, command, co-ordinate and control' · Taylor's definition focuses on 'knowing what you want to do and seeing it done in the best and cheapest way' · Drucker called management a multi-purpose organ that manages a business, managers, and workers"],
[1,"Henry Fayol defined management as the ability to:",["Get things done through others by optimising resources","Forecast, plan, organise, command, co-ordinate and control","Know what you want to do and see it done in the best and cheapest way","Coordinate, motivate and control efforts of others toward stated objectives"],1,"Fayol's classic definition lists six activities: forecast, plan, organise, command, co-ordinate and control.","Ebook","Other options: Koontz's definition focuses on 'getting things done through people in formally organised groups' · Taylor's definition emphasises 'best and cheapest way' — scientific efficiency focus · George Terry's definition mentions 'planning, organising, actuating and controlling' (POAC)"],
[1,"F.W. Taylor defined management as:",["A social process for effective and efficient planning and regulation of enterprise operations","A distinct process of planning, organising, actuating and controlling","The art of knowing what you want to do and seeing it done in the best and cheapest way","Getting things done through and with people in formally organised groups"],2,"Taylor's definition reflects his scientific management focus — knowing exactly what to do and achieving it most efficiently and cheaply.","Ebook","Other options: E.F.L. Breech's definition is 'social process for effective and efficient planning and regulation' · George Terry used 'distinct process of planning, organising, actuating and controlling' · Koontz's definition is about 'getting things done through and with people'"],
[1,"Peter Drucker described management as:",["The task of planning, coordinating, motivating and controlling efforts of others","A multi-purpose organ that manages a business, a manager, and workers and work","The art of getting things done through and with people in formally organised groups","A social process for effective and efficient planning and regulation"],1,"Drucker viewed management as a multi-purpose organ with three distinct functions: managing a business, managing managers, and managing workers and work.","Ebook","Other options: J. Lundy's definition is 'task of planning, coordinating, motivating and controlling' · Koontz's definition focuses on 'formally organised groups' · E.F.L. Breech uses 'social process' in his definition"],
[1,"The acronym POSDCORB was given by:",["Henry Fayol","Harold Koontz","Luther Gullick","George Terry"],2,"Luther Gullick coined POSDCORB — Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Co-ordinating, Reporting, Budgeting.","Ebook","Other options: Henry Fayol's acronym is effectively POSDC (Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Controlling) · George Terry used POAC (Planning, Organising, Actuating, Controlling) · Harold Koontz did not coin a specific acronym for management functions"],
[1,"Which function of management is described as the 'life-spark' that actuates the organisation?",["Planning","Organising","Staffing","Directing"],3,"Directing is called the 'life-spark' of management — it activates the organisation through supervision, motivation, leadership, and communication.","Ebook","Other options: Planning is described as 'bridging the gap from where we are to where we want to be' (Koontz) · Organising is 'bringing together physical, financial and human resources' (Fayol) · Controlling measures accomplishment vs standards and corrects deviations"],
[1,"The correct sequence of steps in the Controlling function of management is:",["Measure actual → Set standard → Compare → Corrective action","Set standard → Measure actual → Compare actual vs standard → Corrective action","Compare → Set standard → Measure actual → Corrective action","Set standard → Compare → Measure actual → Corrective action"],1,"Controlling follows a 4-step sequence: Set standard → Measure actual performance → Compare actual vs standard to find deviation → Take corrective action.","Ebook","Other options: Standards must be set BEFORE measurement — you cannot measure without a benchmark · Corrective action is always the final step, not the first · Comparison happens after both standard is set AND actual is measured"],
[1,"At which level of management is Technical skill MOST critical?",["Top level","Middle level","Lower level","All levels equally"],2,"According to Katz's model, Technical skill (ability to perform specific tasks) is most important at the Lower level, where managers directly supervise hands-on work.","Ebook","Other options: Conceptual skill is most important at the Top level (big-picture thinking) · Human skill is important at ALL levels — not just one level · Middle level managers need a balance of all three skills"],
[1,"Which of Katz's three managerial skills is equally critical at ALL levels of management?",["Technical skill","Human skill","Conceptual skill","Diagnostic skill"],1,"Human skill — the ability to work with people, lead, motivate, and communicate — is essential at all managerial levels.","Ebook","Other options: Technical skill is most important at the lower level · Conceptual skill is most important at the top level · Diagnostic skill is not part of Katz's three-skill framework"],
[1,"Mintzberg's Figurehead role belongs to which category of managerial roles?",["Informational","Decisional","Interpersonal","Operational"],2,"Figurehead is an Interpersonal role — the manager represents the organisation in social, ceremonial, and legal responsibilities and is a source of inspiration.","Ebook","Other options: Monitor, Disseminator, and Spokesperson are the three Informational roles · Entrepreneur, Disturbance Handler, Resource Allocator, and Negotiator are Decisional roles · 'Operational' is not one of Mintzberg's three role categories"],
[1,"Which of the following is a Decisional role in Mintzberg's framework?",["Monitor","Liaison","Spokesperson","Resource Allocator"],3,"Resource Allocator (deciding best use of funding, staff, and resources) is a Decisional role.","Ebook","Other options: Monitor is an Informational role — seeks information about the environment · Liaison is an Interpersonal role — networks with internal and external contacts · Spokesperson is an Informational role — transmits organisational goals to outside world"],
[1,"The Disturbance Handler role in Mintzberg's framework involves:",["Seeking information about the organisation's environment","Creating and controlling change and implementing new ideas","Managing unexpected roadblocks and mediating disputes","Directing important negotiations within or outside the organisation"],2,"The Disturbance Handler manages unexpected roadblocks and mediates disputes — it is a reactive Decisional role.","Ebook","Other options: Entrepreneur is the proactive Decisional role — creates and controls change · Monitor is the Informational role that seeks environmental information · Negotiator directs important negotiations — also a Decisional role but different from Disturbance Handler"],
[1,"Which statement best describes management as a SCIENCE?",["Management uses personal skill and creativity to coordinate people","Management principles are evolved through observation and are universally applicable","Management is result-oriented and aims at maximum productivity at lowest cost","Management is perfected through regular practice and creative application"],1,"Management as science is characterised by a systematised body of knowledge built through continued observation, with universally applicable principles and cause-effect relationships.","Ebook","Other options: Personal skill, creativity, and regular practice describe management as an ART · Result-oriented focus and maximum productivity are also features of management as an art · Management is classified as an inexact or social science — not a pure or exact science"],
[1,"Dean Teele's view on management as art vs. science was:",["100% science — all decisions must be evidence-based","Equally split — 50% art, 50% science","90% art, 10% science today — moving towards 80% art, 20% science","Primarily a social science with no artistic component"],2,"Dean Teele stated management is currently 90% art and 10% science, but moving toward a 80:20 ratio as scientific methods improve.","Ebook","Other options: The direction is toward MORE science, not less — science component is growing · Management is NOT an exact science — it is an inexact/social science · The current ratio is 90:10 (art:science), not 50:50 or equal"],
[1,"Staffing, as a function of management, involves all of the following EXCEPT:",["Recruitment and selection","Training and development","Setting performance standards and measuring deviations","Performance appraisal and promotions"],2,"Setting performance standards and measuring deviations is part of the Controlling function, not Staffing.","Ebook","Other options: Manpower planning, Recruitment, Selection, Training, Remuneration, Appraisal, and Promotions are all Staffing sub-functions · Directing involves supervision, motivation, leadership, and communication — not staffing activities · Organising involves bringing together resources, not managing people processes"],
[1,"The Planning function of management is best described by which Koontz quote?",["'Provide everything useful for functioning'","'Bridges gap from where we are to where we want to be'","'Putting right man on right job'","'Checking progress toward objectives'"],1,"Koontz described Planning as the function that 'bridges the gap from where we are to where we want to be' — it is about deciding in advance what, when, and how.","Ebook","Other options: 'Provide everything useful for functioning' is Fayol's description of Organising · 'Putting right man on right job' describes the Staffing function · 'Checking progress toward objectives' is Haimann's description of Controlling"],
[1,"Middle-level management is primarily associated with which management functions?",["Planning and Coordinating","Organising and Directing","Directing and Controlling","Staffing and Planning"],1,"Middle-level managers (branch/departmental managers) focus primarily on Organising and Directing — executing top-level plans and guiding lower-level managers.","Ebook","Other options: Top management focuses on Planning and Coordinating — setting objectives and policies · Lower management focuses on Directing and Controlling — assigning tasks and maintaining quality · Staffing and Planning together is not the primary focus of any single level"],
[1,"Which level of management is primarily accountable to shareholders?",["Lower management","Middle management","Top management","All levels equally"],2,"Top management (Board of Directors, CEO, MD) sets objectives and policies and is accountable to shareholders for organisational performance.","Ebook","Other options: Lower management (supervisors, foremen) is accountable to middle management, not shareholders · Middle management is accountable to top management, not shareholders directly · Accountability to shareholders is a specific function of top-level governance"],
[1,"Mintzberg's Disseminator role involves:",["Seeking information about the environment and watching for change","Passing useful information to colleagues and team members","Transmitting organisational goals to the outside world","Representing the organisation in ceremonial and legal responsibilities"],1,"The Disseminator passes useful information obtained as Monitor to colleagues and team — it is an internal information-sharing Informational role.","Ebook","Other options: Monitor is the role that SEEKS information from the environment · Spokesperson TRANSMITS organisational goals to the OUTSIDE world · Figurehead handles ceremonial and legal representation — an Interpersonal role"],
[1,"The Entrepreneur role in Mintzberg's framework refers to a manager who:",["Directs important negotiations within or outside the organisation","Decides the best use of funding, staff, and resources","Creates and controls change by generating and implementing new ideas","Networks with internal and external contacts to build relationships"],2,"The Entrepreneur is a proactive Decisional role — the manager creates and controls change by generating and implementing new ideas.","Ebook","Other options: Negotiator directs important negotiations — a different Decisional role · Resource Allocator decides best use of resources — also Decisional but different · Liaison is the networking Interpersonal role"],
[1,"Which of the following is NOT a sub-function of Staffing?",["Manpower planning","Recruitment","Corrective action","Performance appraisal"],2,"Corrective action is the final step of Controlling, not a sub-function of Staffing. Staffing includes manpower planning, recruitment, selection, training, remuneration, appraisal, and promotions.","Ebook","Other options: Training and development IS a staffing sub-function · Remuneration (pay) is part of staffing, not directing · Promotions and transfers are part of the staffing function"],
[1,"Management principles are said to 'guide research' because they:",["Define the scope and limits of a manager's role","Indicate lines of inquiry to make management more effective","Allow training of future managers in specific skills","Enhance efficiency by guiding operations in different situations"],1,"One importance of management principles is that they guide research by indicating directions for further inquiry to make management more effective.","Ebook","Other options: 'Define scope and limits of manager's role' describes the importance of comprehending the nature of management · 'Training future managers' is a separate importance — Fayol stated this specifically · 'Enhance efficiency' refers to the importance of guiding operations"],
[2,"Who is known as the 'Father of Modern Computing' who also pioneered division of labour and profit-sharing plans?",["Robert Owen","Charles Babbage","Henry Robinson Towne","Andrew Ure"],1,"Charles Babbage, known as the 'Father of modern computing,' also promoted division of labour, work specialisation, and the profit-sharing plan that became the basis of the Scanlon Plan.","Ebook","Other options: Robert Owen was the HR pioneer — worker welfare, reduced working hours, no child labour · Henry Robinson Towne introduced 'gain sharing' and systematic management practices · Andrew Ure advocated academic training for factory managers"],
[2,"The problem of 'soldiering' that Taylor's Scientific Management aimed to solve refers to:",["Workers spending too much time on military-style drills during work hours","Workers deliberately working slowly due to fear of job loss, faulty wages, and outdated methods","Managers failing to set fair performance standards","Excessive overtime leading to worker fatigue and low output"],1,"Soldiering was the practice of workers deliberately slowing down work due to fear of job loss if they produced too much, combined with faulty wage systems and outdated work methods.","Ebook","Other options: Taylor's solution was to replace rule-of-thumb with scientific methods and set fair standards · Differential piece-rate was one key tool to align worker incentives · Functional foremanship was another tool — 8 specialist supervisors instead of one generalist"],
[2,"Taylor's principle of 'Harmony, Not Discord' required:",["Workers to cooperate in setting performance standards","Both management and labour to develop a 'Mental Revolution' — seeing each other as indispensable","Scientific selection of workers for every task","Replacement of rule-of-thumb with scientifically tested techniques"],1,"Harmony, Not Discord required a 'Mental Revolution' — both management and labour had to change their outlook and see each other as indispensable partners rather than opponents.","Ebook","Other options: 'Cooperation, Not Individualism' involves joint standard-setting by managers and workers · 'Development of Each Person' involves scientific selection and training · 'Science, Not Rule of Thumb' is about replacing guesswork with experiments"],
[2,"Taylor's Functional Foremanship system is criticised primarily for violating which of Fayol's principles?",["Esprit de Corps","Unity of Direction","Unity of Command","Scalar Chain"],2,"Functional Foremanship — where one worker reports to 8 specialist bosses — violates Fayol's Unity of Command principle, which states one employee should have only one direct supervisor.","Ebook","Other options: Unity of Direction states one plan, one manager for same-objective teams — different from Unity of Command · Scalar Chain is about the chain of command from top to bottom — also related but not the primary violation · Esprit de Corps is about team spirit — not directly violated by foremanship"],
[2,"Under Taylor's Differential Piece-Rate System, a worker who fails to meet the standard receives:",["No pay for that period","A higher rate to encourage improvement","A lower rate per piece produced","Standard rate regardless of output"],2,"Under the Differential Piece-Rate, workers who underperform receive a lower rate per piece, while those who meet or exceed the standard receive a higher rate — creating an incentive to be productive.","Ebook","Other options: Workers are still paid — just at a lower rate, not zero · The higher rate is the REWARD for meeting/exceeding standards, not the penalty · This system links pay directly to output — unlike flat time-based wages"],
[2,"Henri Fayol is referred to as the:",["Father of Scientific Management","Father of Human Relations Movement","Father of Modern Management","Father of Bureaucratic Theory"],2,"Henri Fayol is called the 'Father of Modern Management' for his 14 principles and identification of management as a distinct discipline.","Ebook","Other options: F.W. Taylor is the 'Father of Scientific Management' · Elton Mayo is the 'Father of Human Relations Movement' · Max Weber is the 'Father of Bureaucratic Management Theory'"],
[2,"Fayol's principle of 'Unity of Direction' states that:",["Each employee should receive orders from only one supervisor","There should be one plan and one manager for all activities with the same objective","Individual interests must be subordinated to the general interest","Authority and responsibility must always be balanced"],1,"Unity of Direction means a team working toward the same objective should have one plan and one manager — ensuring coordinated effort.","Ebook","Other options: 'Unity of Command' means one employee, one supervisor — different from Unity of Direction · 'Subordination of Individual to General Interest' is Principle 6 — not about direction · 'Authority and Responsibility' balance is Principle 2, not Unity of Direction"],
[2,"Fayol's principle of 'Scalar Chain' refers to:",["Fair pay including financial and non-financial components","A clear chain of command running from the top to the bottom of the organisation","Balancing central decision-making with employee empowerment","Right place for everything and everyone in a clean, safe workplace"],1,"Scalar Chain is Fayol's 9th principle — a clear, unbroken chain of authority from the highest to the lowest rank in the organisation.","Ebook","Other options: 'Remuneration' (Principle 7) covers fair pay — financial and non-financial · 'Centralisation' (Principle 8) involves the balance between central and employee decision-making · 'Order' (Principle 10) is about right place for everything — clean, safe workplace"],
[2,"Which of Fayol's 14 principles states that individual interest must be subordinated to the interest of the group?",["Equity","Esprit de Corps","Subordination of Individual to General Interest","Unity of Command"],2,"Principle 6 — Subordination of Individual to General Interest — states that the group's interest must always take priority over any individual's interest.","Ebook","Other options: Equity (Principle 11) is about fairness and kindness to all staff — not about group vs individual interest · Esprit de Corps (Principle 14) promotes team spirit and unity · Unity of Command (Principle 4) is about one employee having one direct supervisor"],
[2,"'Esprit de Corps' as Fayol's 14th principle means:",["Giving employees freedom to create and carry out plans","Minimising employee turnover and planning personnel needs","Promoting team spirit and unity within the organisation","Making decisions based on fairness and kindness"],2,"Esprit de Corps (Principle 14) means promoting team spirit and unity — Fayol believed in the strength of teams over individual achievement.","Ebook","Other options: 'Initiative' (Principle 13) gives employees freedom to create and carry out plans · 'Stability of Tenure' (Principle 12) is about minimising turnover · 'Equity' (Principle 11) is about fairness and kindness"],
[2,"Max Weber's Bureaucratic Theory is based on the belief that bureaucracy is:",["Too rigid and therefore ineffective in modern organisations","More rational and efficient than traditional or charismatic systems","Best suited only for small organisations with simple structures","Focused primarily on worker welfare and social relationships"],1,"Weber believed bureaucracy — with its formal rules, hierarchy, and impersonality — was more rational and efficient than traditional or charismatic systems of authority.","Ebook","Other options: Critics of Weber argue bureaucracy is too rigid and resistant to change — but Weber himself saw it as the ideal form · Bureaucracy is typically associated with LARGE organisations, not small ones · Worker welfare and social relationships are the focus of the Human Relations approach (Mayo, Follett)"],
[2,"Which principle of Weber's Bureaucracy requires that all decisions be formally documented?",["Formal Hierarchy Structure","Administrative Class","Official Records","Impersonal Relationships"],2,"Weber's principle of Official Records requires that all administrative decisions and actions be formally documented, creating a filing system that serves as legal evidence.","Ebook","Other options: Formal Hierarchy Structure is about ranked positions answering to levels above · Administrative Class refers to paid full-time employees selected on competence · Impersonal Relationships means decisions based on rational factors, not emotions"],
[2,"Mary Parker Follett is known as the:",["Father of Human Relations Movement","Mother of Modern Management","Father of Modern Management","Pioneer of Bureaucratic Theory"],1,"Mary Parker Follett is called the 'Mother of Modern Management' for her ideas on participative management, integration, and power-with rather than power-over.","Ebook","Other options: Elton Mayo is the 'Father of Human Relations Movement' — distinct from Follett · Henry Fayol is the 'Father of Modern Management' · Max Weber is associated with Bureaucratic Theory"],
[2,"Mary Parker Follett's concept of 'Power WITH' means:",["Power derived from formal position in the hierarchy","Coercive authority exercised over subordinates","Co-active shared power rather than hierarchy-based domination","Power delegated from top management downward"],2,"Follett advocated 'Power WITH' (co-active) rather than 'Power OVER' — shared power across members of the group, not hierarchical domination.","Ebook","Other options: 'Power OVER' is what Follett opposed — traditional hierarchical authority · Formal positional authority is a classical management concept Follett was reacting against · Group power, not individual power, was Follett's central idea"],
[2,"The Hawthorne studies were conducted at which company?",["General Motors, Detroit","Ford Motors, Michigan","Western Electric Company, Hawthorne Plant, Chicago","US Steel Corporation, Pennsylvania"],2,"Elton Mayo and his team conducted the Hawthorne experiments at Western Electric Company's Hawthorne Plant in Chicago between 1927 and 1933.","Ebook","Other options: The Hawthorne EFFECT (named after the plant) shows that workers perform better when observed · Mayo is the researcher; Western Electric was the host company · The study period was 1927–1933, post-WWI, pre-WWII era"],
[2,"The key finding of the Relay Assembly Test Room experiment was:",["Physical lighting conditions are the primary driver of productivity","Productivity increased even when improvements were withdrawn — socio-psychological factors are key","Informal groups naturally set production quotas lower than standards","Worker fatigue is the main cause of productivity decline"],1,"The Relay Assembly Test Room showed productivity increased even when improvements (rest breaks, hours) were withdrawn — proving that recognition, participation, and group dynamics mattered more than physical conditions.","Ebook","Other options: The Illumination experiment tested physical lighting — results showed lighting was NOT the primary driver · The Bank Wiring Observation Room revealed informal production norms and group social dynamics · Socio-psychological factors (recognition, participation) were the KEY discovery, not physical factors"],
[2,"Which Hawthorne conclusion states that workers respond as group members, not just as individuals?",["Interpersonal relations matter more than physical conditions","Factory is a techno-economic and psycho-social unit","Workers are not just economic men — they respond to recognition","Workers react as group members, not individuals"],3,"One of the 7 key Hawthorne conclusions was that workers react as group members — their behaviour is influenced by group norms, not just individual self-interest.","Ebook","Other options: All options are valid Hawthorne conclusions — the question asks specifically about the GROUP MEMBER finding · The 'psycho-social unit' conclusion recognises both technical AND social dimensions · Informal leaders (Conclusion 6) setting group norms is related but a separate conclusion"],
[2,"In Maslow's hierarchy, which need is at the base (MOST basic)?",["Safety needs","Esteem needs","Physiological needs","Love and belonging needs"],2,"Physiological needs (air, food, water) are at the base of Maslow's hierarchy — they must be satisfied before any higher-level needs can be addressed.","Ebook","Other options: Safety needs (shelter, protection) are the SECOND level, not the base · The hierarchy bottom to top: Physiological → Safety → Love/Belonging → Esteem → Self-Actualization · Self-Actualization is at the TOP (peak) of the hierarchy — the hardest to achieve"],
[2,"According to McGregor, Theory X managers believe workers:",["Are self-directed if committed to organisational goals","View work as natural and relaxing as rest or play","Dislike work and need threats, coercion, and punishment","Learn to seek responsibility under the right conditions"],2,"Theory X holds a negative/pessimistic view: workers dislike work, avoid responsibility, and need close supervision with threats and punishment to perform.","Ebook","Other options: All positive statements (self-directed, work is natural, seeks responsibility) belong to Theory Y · McGregor himself preferred Theory Y — he saw it as more realistic and motivating · Theory X is linked to lower Maslow levels (physiological, safety); Theory Y to higher levels"],
[2,"Argyris's Maturity-Immaturity Theory argues that formal organisations make workers:",["More mature, independent, and self-controlled","More passive and dependent — keeping them in an immature state","More creative and capable of autonomous decision-making","More focused on long-term goals rather than immediate rewards"],1,"Argyris argued that formal organisational structures (with tight control, narrow tasks) keep workers in an immature state — passive, dependent, and lacking confidence.","Ebook","Other options: Argyris's solution was to expand job responsibilities and allow participation · A mature person in Argyris's framework is active, independent, and self-controlled · Model II organisations are the goal — open to learning, informed choices, risk-taking"],
[2,"Which approach to management uses PERT, CPM, linear programming, and simulation models?",["Human Relations approach","Behavioural approach","Quantitative approach","Systems approach"],2,"The Quantitative approach (post-WWII) applied mathematical models including PERT, CPM, linear programming, and simulation to management decision-making and control.","Ebook","Other options: Human Relations and Behavioural approaches focus on people, motivation, and group dynamics — not mathematical models · Systems approach focuses on open/closed systems, inputs, transformation, outputs, and feedback · Contingency approach focuses on situational factors — no 'one best way'"],
[2,"In Systems Theory, which type of system interacts with its external environment?",["Closed system","Isolated system","Open system","Static system"],2,"An Open system interacts continuously with its external environment — all organisations are open systems. A closed system is relatively self-contained with little external interaction.","Ebook","Other options: A Closed system (e.g., assembly line) has limited external interaction · All organisations are open systems by definition in management theory · The 4 components: Inputs → Transformation → Outputs → Feedback"],
[2,"The Contingency approach to management holds that:",["There is one best universal way to manage all situations","Management principles are fixed and apply equally in all contexts","The optimal management action depends on internal and external situational factors","Mathematical models determine the best management approach"],2,"Contingency (Situational) theory argues there is no single best way — the optimal action is contingent on the specific situation, integrating theory with organisational complexity.","Ebook","Other options: The belief in 'one best way' is a criticism of classical approaches (Taylor, Fayol) — NOT the Contingency view · Mathematical models are the domain of the Quantitative approach · Fixed universal principles characterise the Classical approach"],
[2,"Robert Owen's key contribution to management was:",["Developing the profit-sharing Scanlon Plan","Pioneering worker welfare — reducing hours, banning child labour under 10, improving living conditions","Introducing academic training programmes for factory managers","Developing systematic management practices with gain sharing"],1,"Robert Owen was an early HR pioneer who championed worker welfare: he reduced the working day to 10.5 hours, prohibited child labour under 10, and improved worker living conditions.","Ebook","Other options: Charles Babbage developed the profit-sharing plan that became the Scanlon Plan · Andrew Ure advocated academic training for factory managers · Henry Robinson Towne introduced systematic management and gain sharing"],
[2,"Which of the following is a valid criticism of the Hawthorne Studies?",["They overemphasised the role of physical conditions","They gave too much importance to individual decisions rather than group decisions","Controlled conditions created the Hawthorne Effect, limiting validity","They ignored the role of informal groups in organisations"],2,"A key criticism is that the controlled, observed conditions themselves improved productivity (Hawthorne Effect), limiting the external validity of the findings.","Ebook","Other options: Hawthorne studies are criticised for OVEREMPHASISING human and social aspects — not underemphasising them · They are criticised for overemphasising GROUP decision-making, not individual — opposite of option B · Informal groups were a KEY FINDING of Hawthorne — they were NOT ignored"],
[2,"Which Fayol principle states that an employee should have only ONE direct supervisor?",["Unity of Direction","Scalar Chain","Unity of Command","Division of Work"],2,"Unity of Command (Principle 4) states that each employee should receive orders from one and only one direct supervisor to avoid confusion and conflict.","Ebook","Other options: Unity of Direction is about one plan, one manager for a team with the same objective — applies to the TEAM, not the individual · Scalar Chain is the chain of authority from top to bottom of the entire organisation · Taylor's Functional Foremanship violates Unity of Command by having 8 supervisors"],
[2,"The 4 components of an Organisational System in the correct sequence are:",["Transformation → Inputs → Outputs → Feedback","Inputs → Outputs → Transformation → Feedback","Inputs → Transformation → Outputs → Feedback","Feedback → Inputs → Transformation → Outputs"],2,"The systems model flows: Inputs (money, materials, men, machines) → Transformation Process (managerial and technical abilities) → Outputs (products, services, profits) → Feedback (information about outcomes).","Ebook","Other options: Feedback is always the LAST component — it loops back information about outputs · Transformation is the processing stage, not the first stage · This is analogous to the cardiac cycle: preload (inputs) → cardiac contraction (transformation) → cardiac output → baroreceptor feedback"],
[2,"Fayol's principle of 'Stability of Tenure' primarily aims to:",["Promote team spirit and collaboration","Give employees the freedom to create and implement plans","Minimise employee turnover and plan personnel needs carefully","Ensure fair and consistent treatment of all employees"],2,"Stability of Tenure (Principle 12) recognises that high turnover is costly and disruptive — management should plan personnel needs and minimise unnecessary staff changes.","Ebook","Other options: Esprit de Corps (Principle 14) promotes team spirit · Initiative (Principle 13) gives employees freedom to create and implement plans · Equity (Principle 11) ensures fair and kind treatment"],
[3,"Social responsibility of business is best defined as:",["Maximising profit for shareholders while complying with the law","The obligation of a business to enhance the condition of society along with its own interests","Donating a percentage of profits to charitable causes each year","Complying with government regulations on environmental standards"],1,"Social responsibility is the obligation to enhance the condition of society ALONGSIDE the firm's own interests — it goes beyond profit maximisation and regulatory compliance.","Ebook","Other options: Milton Friedman argued that profit maximisation IS the social responsibility — this is a key 'Against' argument · Donations or compliance alone are insufficient definitions — SR is a broader obligation · Social responsiveness is the ABILITY to implement policies — distinct from the obligation (SR)"],
[3,"Which argument AGAINST social responsibility was championed by economist Milton Friedman?",["Social responsibility reduces government regulation on businesses","Social responsibility adds cost to operations, raising prices and reducing business viability","Social responsibility generates new profit avenues through conservation and recycling","Social responsibility helps build a positive public image that attracts customers"],1,"Milton Friedman argued that social responsibility adds cost to the business, which is passed on as higher prices, contradicting the basic function of a business and reducing its viability.","Ebook","Other options: Reducing government regulation is actually an argument FOR social responsibility (Argument 5 in FOR list) · New profit avenues through recycling is an argument FOR social responsibility (Argument 8) · Building public image is an argument FOR social responsibility (Argument 4)"],
[3,"Which of the following is an argument FOR social responsibility of business?",["Contrary to basic function — adds cost and raises prices","Distortion of resource allocation — diverts resources from optimal use","Business is part of society — subsystem must benefit the whole system","Lack of skills — business people are not trained to handle social problems"],2,"The argument that business is part of society (a subsystem of the larger social system) and must benefit the whole system is a FOR argument — it provides moral justification for SR.","Ebook","Other options: All other three options are arguments AGAINST social responsibility · 'Contrary to basic function' is Friedman's core argument against SR · 'Lack of skills' argues that managers lack competence to handle social issues"],
[3,"Jack Ma's priority order for stakeholders is:",["Shareholders → Employees → Customers","Government → Customers → Employees","Customers → Employees → Investors","Employees → Customers → Shareholders"],2,"Jack Ma famously prioritised Customers first, then Employees, then Investors — the reverse of the traditional shareholder-first view.","Ebook","Other options: Traditional Western corporate view puts Shareholders first — Ma explicitly reversed this · Government is a stakeholder group but not in Ma's priority order · This view aligns with the 'customer is king' and 'employees are biggest asset' philosophies"],
[3,"A Social Audit is defined as:",["A government review of a company's tax compliance and financial records","A systematic study and evaluation of the social rather than economic performance of an organisation","An internal assessment of employee satisfaction and welfare programmes","An annual report published by a company on its charitable donations"],1,"A Social Audit is a systematic study and evaluation of an organisation's SOCIAL performance — distinct from a financial audit which evaluates economic performance.","Ebook","Other options: Social audits can be government-required OR voluntary — it is not exclusively a government activity · Employee satisfaction is one component but not the complete definition · Annual charity reports are a narrow form — a social audit is broader and more systematic"],
[3,"The five steps of a Social Audit in correct order are:",["Monitor → Measure → Evaluate → Frame strategies → Frame programmes","Frame programmes → Monitor → Measure → Evaluate → Frame strategies","Evaluate → Frame programmes → Monitor → Frame strategies → Measure","Frame strategies → Frame programmes → Measure → Monitor → Evaluate"],1,"The 5 steps of a Social Audit are: (1) Frame social programmes, (2) Monitor activities, (3) Measure social performance, (4) Evaluate socially responsive activities, (5) Frame strategies for future goals.","Ebook","Other options: Framing programmes comes FIRST — you must design the programme before monitoring it · Strategy framing comes LAST — it is based on evaluation findings · The sequence mirrors a Plan-Do-Check-Act (PDCA) cycle"],
[3,"Which of the following is NOT one of the six social stakeholder groups?",["Creditors and Suppliers","Competitors","Government","Society"],1,"Competitors are NOT among the 6 stakeholder groups. The 6 are: Shareholders, Employees, Customers, Creditors & Suppliers, Society, and Government.","Ebook","Other options: Creditors and Suppliers ARE one of the 6 groups — they provide raw materials and capital inputs · Society as a stakeholder focuses on health, safety, and economic development · Government's stake involves taxes (corporate, payroll, sales) and GDP contribution"],
[3,"Managerial ethics is best defined as:",["Compliance with government laws and industry regulations","Set of moral principles or beliefs that affect the behaviour of employees","Corporate policies on acceptable business conduct","Social norms that determine appropriate workplace behaviour"],1,"Managerial ethics is the set of moral principles or beliefs — both legal and moral — that affect the behaviour of employees across all organisational levels.","Ebook","Other options: Legal compliance is just the Legal Ethics component — managerial ethics is broader · Corporate policies reflect ethics but are not the definition of ethics itself · Ethics includes moral principles that go BEYOND what laws and social norms require"],
[3,"Offering paternity leave (not legally required) is an example of:",["Legal ethics","Moral ethics","Corporate governance","Social audit"],1,"Moral ethics goes beyond the legal minimum — offering paternity leave when not legally required reflects moral commitment to employee welfare beyond compliance.","Ebook","Other options: Legal ethics involves fair hiring, safe workplace, fair pay, OSHA compliance — things required by law · Corporate governance is about board oversight and accountability — not individual HR decisions · Social audit evaluates social performance broadly — not a specific policy decision"],
[3,"Kohlberg's Stage 4 (Law and Order) is significant because:",["It represents the highest level of moral development achievable","It is the stage where individuals follow abstract moral principles like equality and dignity","Most active members of society operate at this stage","It is where individuals act purely for self-interest and immediate rewards"],2,"Stage 4 is where individuals follow rules because they maintain social order — it is the stage at which MOST active members of society operate.","Ebook","Other options: Stage 6 (Universal Ethical Principles) is the highest level — rare and abstract · Stage 6 involves abstract principles like equality and dignity, not Stage 4 · Pure self-interest describes Stage 2 (Instrumental Orientation) — pre-conventional level"],
[3,"In Kohlberg's framework, Stage 6 (Universal Ethical Principles) is characterised by:",["Obedience to avoid punishment","Seeking approval from others to avoid disapproval","Following laws as social contracts that can be changed if unjust","Acting on abstract principles of equality and dignity — even disobeying unjust laws"],3,"Stage 6 is the highest level — individuals are guided by abstract universal principles (equality, human dignity, justice) and may even disobey unjust laws based on conscience.","Ebook","Other options: Obedience to avoid punishment is Stage 1 — the lowest pre-conventional stage · Seeking approval describes Stage 3 (Good Boy/Nice Girl) — conventional level · Stage 5 (Social Contract) views laws as changeable social contracts — Stage 6 goes further to universal principles"],
[3,"Which of the following is a criticism of Kohlberg's theory?",["It overemphasises the role of punishment in moral development","It is biased toward Western and male perspectives (Carol Gilligan's critique)","It fails to identify any stages of moral development","It ignores the role of social norms in shaping moral behaviour"],1,"Carol Gilligan criticised Kohlberg's framework as biased toward Western, male perspectives — it also ignores other values beyond justice and doesn't account for inconsistency in moral judgements.","Ebook","Other options: Kohlberg DOES identify 6 stages — the criticism is about their cultural bias, not absence of stages · Punishment plays a role only in Stage 1 — Kohlberg's framework spans well beyond punishment · Social norms are part of the Conventional level (Stages 3 and 4) in Kohlberg's model"],
[3,"Which of the following is an Objective of a Social Audit?",["Maximise shareholder return on investment","Assess gaps between social needs and available resources","Determine legal compliance with environmental regulations","Calculate the economic profit from social programmes"],1,"One of the 5 objectives of a Social Audit is to assess gaps between social needs and available resources — helping organisations prioritise their social commitments.","Ebook","Other options: Maximising shareholder return is a financial goal — opposite of what a social audit evaluates · Legal compliance is an objective of a financial/regulatory audit, not a social audit · Economic profit is measured by financial analysis — a social audit focuses on social impact"],
[3,"The argument that 'legal provisions cannot keep pace with change, so business must fill the gap' is an argument:",["Against social responsibility — adds unnecessary cost","For social responsibility — business must maintain society","For social responsibility — avoidance of government regulation","Against social responsibility — distorts resource allocation"],1,"Argument 6 FOR social responsibility states that since laws lag behind social change, businesses must proactively fill the gap and maintain societal standards.","Ebook","Other options: The opposite argument (AGAINST) is that SR replaces self-interest and reduces efficiency · 'Avoidance of government regulation' is Argument 5 FOR — socially responsible firms attract less regulatory attention · Distortion of resource allocation is a separate argument AGAINST"],
[3,"The 5 factors affecting managerial ethics do NOT include:",["Stage of moral development","Organisational culture","Profitability of the firm","Intensity of the ethical issue"],2,"The 5 factors are: Stage of moral development, Individual characteristics, Structural variables (formal rules), Organisational culture, and Intensity of the ethical issue. Profitability is NOT one of them.","Ebook","Other options: Individual characteristics (personal values, personality) IS one of the 5 factors · Structural variables include formal rules and regulations — these shape ethical behaviour · Intensity of the ethical issue (how serious the moral stakes are) directly affects ethical decisions"],
[3,"Kohlberg's 'Good Boy / Nice Girl' orientation (Stage 3) is characterised by:",["Following laws to maintain social order and fulfil duty","Acting based on self-interest — 'What's in it for me?'","Seeking approval and behaving well to avoid disapproval from others","Applying abstract principles of justice regardless of rules"],2,"Stage 3 is about seeking social approval — behaving well to be seen as a 'good person' and avoiding disapproval from peers and authority figures.","Ebook","Other options: Stage 4 (Law and Order) is about following rules to maintain social order · Stage 2 (Instrumental Orientation) is the self-interest stage — 'What's in it for me?' · Stage 6 (Universal Ethical Principles) involves abstract justice principles"],
[3,"Social responsiveness refers to:",["The obligation of a business to society beyond its own interests","The ability of a firm to implement policies and take part in activities that benefit both society and the firm","A systematic evaluation of a company's social performance","The measurement of corporate donations and volunteering hours"],1,"Social responsiveness is the ABILITY to implement policies and activities that benefit both society and the firm — it is the operational/implementation side of social responsibility.","Ebook","Other options: 'Obligation to society' is the definition of social RESPONSIBILITY — different from responsiveness · Systematic evaluation of social performance is the definition of a Social AUDIT · Measurement of donations is a narrow component of social responsiveness measurement"],
[3,"Which ethical guideline states that 'the best way is prevention'?",["Integrate ethics with management practices","Value forgiveness — support people in correcting mistakes","Codes of ethics minimise unethical behaviour before it occurs","Make ethics decisions in groups for better quality and credibility"],2,"Guideline 3 states 'the best way is prevention' — codes of ethics and proactive ethical frameworks prevent unethical behaviour from arising rather than reacting to it.","Ebook","Other options: 'Integrate ethics with management practices' is Guideline 5 — embedding ethics in strategy and HR policies · 'Value forgiveness' is Guideline 7 — supporting people who make mistakes and try to correct them · 'Make ethics decisions in groups' is Guideline 4 — better quality and more credibility through diversity"],
[3,"Problems with Social Audits include all EXCEPT:",["Attitude of the auditor affecting results","Time lag in impact assessment","Provides cost-benefit data for social programmes","Inadequate information available for measurement"],2,"Providing cost-benefit data is a MERIT of Social Audits, not a problem. Problems include auditor attitude, poor programme design, inadequate information, and time lag in seeing impact.","Ebook","Other options: Auditor attitude is a genuine problem — subjective bias can distort evaluation · Time lag is a real problem — social impact may take years to appear · Poor programme design makes it difficult to measure outcomes accurately"],
[3,"The Employees stakeholder group's primary stake in a business is:",["Return on investment and timely financial information","Revenue generation and supply of raw materials","Income, benefits, health and safety in the workplace","GDP contribution and corporate tax revenues"],2,"Employees — described as the business's biggest asset — have a primary stake in income, benefits, and their health and safety at work.","Ebook","Other options: Return on investment and financial information are the stakes of SHAREHOLDERS · Revenue generation and raw materials are the stakes of CREDITORS AND SUPPLIERS · GDP and taxes are the stakes of GOVERNMENT"],
[3,"Which of the following best describes Level 1 (Pre-Conventional) of Kohlberg's moral development?",["Behaviour driven by social approval and following laws for social order","Behaviour driven by avoiding punishment and self-interest","Behaviour guided by universal ethical principles of justice and dignity","Behaviour based on viewing laws as social contracts that can be changed"],1,"Pre-Conventional (Stages 1 and 2) is the lowest level — Stage 1 avoids punishment, Stage 2 acts from self-interest ('What's in it for me?'). Both are external, self-focused drivers.","Ebook","Other options: Social approval and law-following describe Level 2 (Conventional) — Stages 3 and 4 · Universal principles describe Level 3 (Post-Conventional) — Stage 6 · Laws as social contracts describe Stage 5 — the lower part of Post-Conventional level"],
[3,"Which argument FOR social responsibility states that business has power and therefore must have corresponding responsibility?",["Long-term self-interest","Balance of power and responsibility","Best use of business resources","Moral justification"],1,"Argument 7 FOR social responsibility: Business has significant power in society — and with great power comes corresponding responsibility to use it for social good.","Ebook","Other options: Long-term self-interest (Argument 2) says business existence depends on satisfying stakeholders · Best use of resources (Argument 9) is about optimal use of managerial skills and capital · Moral justification (Argument 3) says taking from society obligates giving back"],
[4,"Span of Control is defined as:",["The total number of employees in an organisation","The maximum number of subordinates a single manager can effectively supervise","The number of management levels in an organisation's hierarchy","The range of decisions a manager is authorised to make"],1,"Span of Control (also called Span of Management or Span of Supervision) is the maximum number of subordinates a single executive/manager can effectively supervise.","Ebook","Other options: Total employees is an organisational size metric — not span of control · Number of management levels describes the height of an organisation (tall vs flat) · Range of decision authority relates to decentralisation and delegation — different concepts"],
[4,"At the executive level, classical theory recommends an ideal span of:",["3–5 subordinates","5–8 subordinates","10–15 subordinates","15–25 subordinates"],1,"Classical theory recommends 5–8 subordinates at the executive level. At first-line supervision (operational level), the recommended span is 15–25.","Ebook","Other options: 15–25 is the recommended span at FIRST-LINE supervision level, not executive level · Graicunas specifically concluded 5–6 as optimal based on his relationship formula · As hierarchy level decreases (toward front-line), the appropriate span INCREASES"],
[4,"A WIDE span of control leads to which type of organisational structure?",["Tall organisation with many layers","Flat organisation with fewer levels","Matrix organisation","Functional organisation"],1,"A wide span of control means each manager supervises many subordinates — requiring fewer levels of management, creating a flat (horizontal) organisation.","Ebook","Other options: A NARROW span creates a TALL organisation — more levels needed because each manager supervises fewer people · Wide span = flat = fewer managers = lower cost = faster communication · Matrix and functional describe organisational TYPES, not the span-structure relationship"],
[4,"Which of the following is an advantage of a FLAT (wide span) organisation?",["Rapid communication within small, tightly-managed teams","More opportunities for employee promotion","Agility — faster communication and decision-making","Greater degree of specialisation and division of labour"],2,"Flat organisations with wide spans are more agile — fewer levels mean faster communication flows and quicker decision-making throughout the organisation.","Ebook","Other options: Rapid communication in small teams and greater specialisation are advantages of TALL (narrow span) organisations · More promotion opportunities is a TALL organisation advantage — more layers mean more rungs to climb · Wide span organisations have HIGHER managerial workload — a disadvantage"],
[4,"A disadvantage of a TALL (narrow span) organisation is:",["High managerial workload per manager","Role confusion more likely among employees","Communication is slow — too many layers hampers decision-making","Possible cultivation of distrust toward management"],2,"In a tall organisation, messages must pass through many levels — this slows communication significantly and hampers timely decision-making.","Ebook","Other options: High managerial workload and role confusion are disadvantages of FLAT (wide span) organisations · Distrust of management is also a potential flat organisation disadvantage · Tall organisations develop departmental SILOS — a related but distinct disadvantage"],
[4,"V.A. Graicunas was a:",["American industrial engineer who studied worker fatigue","French management consultant who developed a mathematical relationship formula","German sociologist who developed Bureaucratic Theory","British economist who proposed division of labour"],1,"V.A. Graicunas was a French management consultant who developed a mathematical formula to analyse the exponential growth of superior-subordinate relationships as span increases.","Ebook","Other options: Taylor was the American industrial engineer who studied worker output and fatigue · Max Weber was the German sociologist who developed Bureaucratic Theory · Charles Babbage (not Graicunas) pioneered division of labour concepts"],
[4,"In Graicunas's theory, 'Direct Single' relationships refer to:",["Relationships between the superior and each possible combination of subordinates","Relationships between subordinates themselves","Relationships between the superior and each individual subordinate","The total count of all relationships in the span"],2,"Direct Single relationships are the simplest — one-to-one between the superior and each individual subordinate. With n subordinates, there are exactly n direct single relationships.","Ebook","Other options: Direct GROUP relationships are between the superior and each possible COMBINATION of subordinates · CROSS relationships are peer-to-peer relationships between subordinates themselves · The TOTAL formula combines all three types: n(2^n/2 + n - 1)"],
[4,"Graicunas's key finding was that as the number of subordinates increases, relationships:",["Increase linearly — doubling subordinates doubles relationships","Increase arithmetically — each new subordinate adds a fixed number of relationships","Increase exponentially — even adding one more subordinate dramatically multiplies relationships","Remain constant — relationships are determined by organisational culture, not headcount"],2,"Graicunas showed that relationships grow EXPONENTIALLY — adding just one more subordinate (e.g., from 5 to 6) creates a disproportionately large increase in total relationships.","Ebook","Other options: The exponential growth is why Graicunas concluded 5–6 is the optimal span · Linear or arithmetic growth would make span of control far less important · The formula Total = n(2^n/2 + n - 1) demonstrates the exponential nature"],
[4,"Graicunas concluded that the most reasonable span of control is:",["3–4 subordinates","5–6 subordinates","8–10 subordinates","12–15 subordinates"],1,"Based on his mathematical analysis of relationship growth, Graicunas concluded that 5 to 6 subordinates is the optimal span of control.","Ebook","Other options: Classical theory says 5–8 at executive level — Graicunas's formula specifically points to 5–6 · First-line supervision can handle 15–25 because work is more routine and standardised · The exponential growth of relationships justifies keeping the span below 7"],
[4,"Which is a valid criticism of Graicunas's Theory?",["It recommends too wide a span of control for executive levels","It ignores the frequency of relationships and the strain they generate","It overestimates the importance of cross-relationships between subordinates","It cannot be used to determine the optimal number of subordinates"],1,"A key criticism is that Graicunas counts the number of relationships but ignores HOW OFTEN those relationships occur and the strain/effort each interaction generates.","Ebook","Other options: Graicunas actually recommends a NARROW span (5–6) — not too wide · The theory is criticised for LEAVING OUT certain possible relationships, not overestimating cross-relationships · The theory can be used to determine optimal span — 5–6 is its specific recommendation"],
[4,"How does the 'Nature of Work' factor affect span of control?",["Routine, standardised work allows a WIDER span; complex, uncertain work requires a NARROWER span","Complex work allows a wider span because managers develop better judgement","Nature of work has no effect — only managerial ability determines span","Routine work requires closer supervision and thus a narrower span"],0,"Routine, standardised work (programmed decisions) requires less supervision — managers can handle more subordinates (wider span). Complex, non-routine work at top levels requires closer supervision (narrower span).","Ebook","Other options: The relationship is intuitive: more complex work = more supervision needed = fewer subordinates · At lower levels, work is more routine → wider span (15–25 at first-line supervision) · At top levels, work is complex and strategic → narrower span (5–8)"],
[4,"When subordinates are highly trained, experienced, and self-confident, the appropriate span of control is:",["Narrower — experienced workers need more monitoring","Wider — capable subordinates need less supervision","Unchanged — subordinate ability does not affect span","Determined solely by organisational policy"],1,"Trained, experienced, self-confident subordinates need less supervision — managers can oversee more of them, allowing a wider span of control.","Ebook","Other options: Counter-intuitive: experienced workers need LESS, not more, supervision · This is analogous to a senior resident needing less supervision than an intern · Higher degree of delegation to capable subordinates also enables wider span"],
[4,"The 'Span of Attention' reason for span of control limits refers to:",["The human energy constraints that limit how many people a manager can manage","The limitation of time — managers cannot supervise all employees personally","The fact that humans can only pay full attention to a limited number of people simultaneously","The communication challenge of maintaining effective contact with large groups"],2,"The Span of Attention limitation means humans have a finite cognitive capacity — they can only pay FULL attention to a limited number of people at any one time.","Ebook","Other options: Limitation of time is a SEPARATE reason (Reason 1) — distinct from span of attention · Limitation of capacity (Reason 2) refers to human energy limits — different from attention · Communication (Reason 4) is also separate — about the quality of interaction, not attention span"],
[4,"Which of the following increases the appropriate span of control for a manager?",["More complex, non-routine work at the top level","Greater degree of decentralisation and delegation","Greater need for personal observation and direct supervision","Higher number of departments under the manager's authority"],1,"A higher degree of decentralisation and delegation allows subordinates to make more decisions independently — reducing the supervisor's oversight burden and enabling a wider span.","Ebook","Other options: Complex work DECREASES the appropriate span — more guidance needed per subordinate · Need for personal observation DECREASES the span — written reports allow wider spans · Number of departments affects organisational structure, but the key determinant is actual supervision burden"],
[4,"Silos developing and preventing cross-functional problem solving is a disadvantage of which type of organisation?",["Flat organisation (wide span)","Tall organisation (narrow span)","Matrix organisation","Both flat and tall equally"],1,"Tall organisations with many specialised layers tend to develop departmental silos — units that operate in isolation, preventing cross-functional collaboration and problem solving.","Ebook","Other options: Flat organisations are more agile and collaborative — silos are LESS likely with fewer levels · Role confusion is a disadvantage of FLAT organisations, not tall ones · Slow communication is the primary tall organisation disadvantage; silos are a secondary consequence"],
[4,"The term 'Span of Management' is also known as:",["Span of Control only","Span of Supervision only","Both Span of Control and Span of Supervision","Neither — these are distinct concepts"],2,"Span of Control, Span of Management, and Span of Supervision are three names for the same concept — the number of subordinates a manager can effectively oversee.","Ebook","Other options: All three terms are interchangeable — used in different textbooks but mean the same thing · Spriegal's definition: 'the number of people reporting directly to an authority' · Classical theory ideal: 5–8 at executive; 15–25 at first-line"],
[4,"Which of the following is a DISADVANTAGE of a flat (wide span) organisation?",["Slow communication through many management layers","Silos developing between departments","High managerial workload for each manager","Fewer promotion opportunities for employees"],2,"In a flat organisation, each manager supervises many subordinates — this creates a high managerial workload, potentially leading to role confusion and distrust if managers are overstretched.","Ebook","Other options: Slow communication and silos are disadvantages of TALL (narrow span) organisations · Fewer promotion opportunities is a flat organisation disadvantage — fewer levels means fewer rungs on the ladder · Both options C and D are flat organisation disadvantages — question asks for ONE that applies"],
[4,"In Graicunas's framework, 'Cross relationships' refer to:",["Relationships between the superior and individual subordinates","Relationships between the superior and groups of subordinates","Relationships between subordinates themselves (peer-to-peer)","Relationships across different management levels"],2,"Cross relationships are peer-to-peer relationships between subordinates — the manager must be aware of and manage these indirect relationships even without directly participating in them.","Ebook","Other options: Superior-to-individual relationships are 'Direct Single' relationships · Superior-to-group combinations are 'Direct Group' relationships · Cross relationships grow fastest as span increases — major contributor to exponential growth"],
[4,"Clear lines of reporting and specific roles enabled by span of control help with:",["Limitation of time","Span of attention","Growth chances — better planning and improvement opportunities","Communication effectiveness"],2,"Reason 6 for importance of span of control is 'Growth Chances' — clear roles and specific reporting structures enable better planning and create improvement opportunities for individuals.","Ebook","Other options: Limitation of time (Reason 1) is about a manager's physical time constraints · Span of attention (Reason 3) is about cognitive capacity · Communication effectiveness (Reason 4) is about quality of interaction with specific individuals"],
[4,"When clear plans, policies, and standing procedures exist in an organisation, the span of control can be:",["Narrower — more guidance is needed from managers","Wider — managers make fewer ad hoc decisions","Eliminated — self-managing teams don't need supervision","Fixed at 5–6 regardless of other factors"],1,"When an organisation has clear plans, policies, and procedures, managers spend less time making individual decisions for each subordinate — freeing capacity to supervise more people (wider span).","Ebook","Other options: More policies mean LESS need for managerial intervention — enabling wider span, not narrower · Even with great policies, some degree of supervision is always needed · The ideal span of 5–6 (Graicunas) or 5–8 (executive level) is a guideline — context always matters"],
[5,"A managerial 'decision' is best defined as:",["A problem that requires analysis before any action is taken","An act requiring judgement that is translated into action","The collection of all available information before selecting a course of action","The process of identifying and evaluating alternative solutions"],1,"A decision is defined as an act requiring judgement that is translated into action — it is distinct from analysis or information gathering, which precede it.","Ebook","Other options: Information gathering is Step 2 of the 8-step process — it precedes the decision · Identifying and evaluating alternatives are Steps 4 and 5 — also preceding the actual decision · Decision-MAKING (the process) is broader than a decision (the act itself)"],
[5,"Which of the following is NOT one of the 4 characteristics of managerial decisions?",["Oriented toward long-range organisational objectives","Best choice from a set of alternatives","Delegated to the most qualified subordinate","Requires commitment of scarce resources"],2,"The 4 characteristics are: oriented toward long-range objectives, best choice from alternatives, involves organisational change, and requires commitment of scarce resources. Delegation is not a defining characteristic.","Ebook","Other options: All three correct characteristics are present in the other options · Delegation is a management function — not a characteristic of decisions · The 4th characteristic is 'involves organisational change' — not delegation"],
[5,"The Rational Decision-Making Model is described as 'prescriptive' because:",["It describes how managers actually make decisions in practice","It prescribes how decisions SHOULD ideally be made using complete information and logic","It prescribes specific solutions for common management problems","It is based on observations of real manager behaviour"],1,"The Rational Model is prescriptive — it sets out the ideal process for how decisions SHOULD be made (complete information, objective analysis, optimal outcome) rather than describing real behaviour.","Ebook","Other options: The Non-Rational model is DESCRIPTIVE — it describes how decisions are actually made in practice · Prescriptive means 'how it should be done'; descriptive means 'how it is actually done' · Real manager behaviour (with cognitive limits, emotions, biases) is captured by the Non-Rational model"],
[5,"Herbert Simon's concept of 'Satisficing' means:",["Optimising — finding the single best solution by evaluating all alternatives","Accepting a good enough solution rather than exhaustively searching for the best","Delegating the decision to a group to ensure the best outcome","Using mathematical models to identify the optimal choice"],1,"Simon's Satisficers simplify choices and accept a 'good enough' solution — they stop searching once a satisfactory option is found, unlike Maximisers who continue seeking the optimal.","Ebook","Other options: Maximisers try to optimise — they take longer and report more regret over decisions · Satisficing is NOT laziness — it is a rational response to the limits of human cognition (bounded rationality) · Simon won the Nobel Prize for his concept of bounded rationality underlying satisficing"],
[5,"Gerd Gigerenzer's contribution to decision-making theory was:",["Maximisers make better decisions than satisficers in all contexts","Simple heuristics based on experience often outperform exhaustive optimisation","Decision trees with probability estimates always identify the optimal choice","Group decisions are superior to individual decisions in all situations"],1,"Gigerenzer showed that simple, experience-based heuristics (mental shortcuts) can lead to better outcomes than exhaustive rational optimisation — especially in uncertain environments.","Ebook","Other options: Simon (not Gigerenzer) developed the maximiser vs satisficer concept · Gigerenzer's work supports heuristics — he argues AGAINST always seeking exhaustive optimisation · Decision trees are a formal quantitative tool — different from Gigerenzer's heuristics approach"],
[5,"In the 8-step decision-making process, Step 3 'Set Criteria' means:",["Generating as many alternative solutions as possible using brainstorming","Defining baseline principles for evaluating alternatives (e.g., profit impact, cultural fit)","Collecting relevant data from internal and external sources","Selecting the alternative that best meets the identified criteria"],1,"Step 3 is about establishing the criteria (profit, culture, feasibility) AGAINST WHICH alternatives will be evaluated — these must be set before alternatives are assessed.","Ebook","Other options: Generating alternatives is Step 4 (Brainstorm) — comes AFTER criteria are set · Collecting data is Step 2 (Gather Information) — comes BEFORE criteria setting · Selecting the alternative is Step 6 — near the end of the process"],
[5,"The FINAL step in the 8-step decision-making process is:",["Execute the decision","Select the best alternative","Evaluate results and learn for future decisions","Brainstorm alternatives using cause-effect diagrams"],2,"Step 8 is Evaluate Results — assess the outcome of the decision, learn from it, and make corrections for future decisions. This closes the decision-making loop.","Ebook","Other options: Execute the decision is Step 7 — converting the decision into an action plan · Select the best alternative is Step 6 — choosing among evaluated options · Brainstorming is Step 4 — early in the process, not the end"],
[5,"Decision-making under RISK is characterised by:",["All alternatives, conditions, and outcomes are known with certainty","Future is completely unpredictable — alternatives, risks, and consequences are unknown","Incomplete information but a good estimate of the probability of each outcome","Only one possible outcome for each alternative"],2,"Under Risk, information is incomplete but the decision-maker has a reasonable estimate of the probability of different outcomes — unlike uncertainty (no probability estimates possible).","Ebook","Other options: Full knowledge of outcomes describes CERTAINTY — e.g., routine operations · Completely unpredictable future with unknown consequences describes UNCERTAINTY · Risk sits BETWEEN certainty and uncertainty on the information spectrum"],
[5,"In a Decision Tree, what does a SQUARE node represent?",["A chance event with uncertain outcomes","A decision point where the manager must choose between alternatives","A probability estimate for a particular outcome","The final payoff or result of a decision path"],1,"In Decision Trees, SQUARES represent decision points (where the manager makes a choice) and CIRCLES represent chance events (where probability determines the outcome).","Ebook","Other options: Chance events are represented by CIRCLES — not squares · Probability estimates are written on the branches connecting nodes, not in the nodes themselves · The final payoff appears at the end of branches — not at decision or chance nodes"],
[5,"Preference/Utility Theory in decision-making under uncertainty recognises that:",["All managers have identical attitudes toward risk","Mathematical models always identify the best risk-reward trade-off","Individual attitudes toward risk vary — some are risk averters, others are risk-takers","Top managers should avoid all high-risk decisions regardless of potential reward"],2,"Preference/Utility Theory acknowledges that risk attitudes vary — 'risk averters' minimise risk while 'gamblers' seek high-risk/high-reward options. Top managers often take the largest risks but typically not with their own money.","Ebook","Other options: Top managers take the LARGEST risks — but usually with the organisation's money, not their own · Mathematical models (Risk Analysis, Decision Trees) are separate approaches under uncertainty · Utility/preference is highly individual — two rational people can make different choices with the same data"],
[5,"A Management Information System (MIS) primarily:",["Helps identify problems and finalise complex, unstructured decisions","Converts raw data into useful information for managers through computer-based reporting","Provides communication tools and documents for semi-structured decisions","Simulates business scenarios to test the impact of different decisions"],1,"MIS converts raw data into structured, useful information for managers — supporting routine, structured decisions through computer-based reporting and coordination.","Ebook","Other options: Helping identify problems and finalise decisions describes a DSS (Decision Support System) · Semi-structured decisions are the domain of DSS — MIS handles ROUTINE, structured decisions · Simulation is a specific OR technique — different from MIS"],
[5,"Group Decision Making is defined as a process where:",["A senior manager consults subordinates but retains sole accountability for the decision","Individuals collectively make a choice with no single person accountable for the final decision","A committee votes on alternatives and the majority choice is implemented","External consultants are brought in to make decisions for the organisation"],1,"Group Decision Making is where individuals collectively make a choice — accountability is distributed across the group, not held by a single person.","Ebook","Other options: If a senior manager retains accountability, it is consultative — not true group decision-making · Majority vote is one method but the definition is broader — it includes consensus and other methods · External consultants would have advisory, not decision-making accountability"],
[5,"'Group Think' is a disadvantage of group decision-making that refers to:",["Too many irrelevant opinions being contributed by peripheral members","Dominant members overusing their authority to control the outcome","Members going along with the group consensus to avoid conflict rather than expressing genuine views","The group taking longer to decide than an individual would"],2,"Group Think is the tendency to conform to group consensus to avoid conflict — members suppress genuine disagreement, leading to poor-quality decisions that appear unanimous.","Ebook","Other options: Irrelevant opinions and dominant members are separate group DM disadvantages · Slow decision-making (time) is a disadvantage but different from Group Think · Group Think was a concept popularised by Irving Janis — linked to major historical decision failures"],
[5,"Which type of group decision-making method uses multiple rounds of questionnaires without face-to-face meetings?",["Interacting groups","Nominal groups","Delphi groups","Consensus groups"],2,"Delphi groups avoid face-to-face interaction — questionnaires are sent, summarised, and recirculated in multiple rounds until consensus emerges. Used for forecasting market trends and future problems.","Ebook","Other options: Interacting groups meet face-to-face — the most common method · Nominal groups work independently in silence — then discuss collectively · Delphi is the ONLY method with NO face-to-face contact at any stage"],
[5,"Nominal Group technique is most useful when the goal is to:",["Build team relationships and social bonds","Rapidly reach consensus in a face-to-face debate","Maximise individual creativity and avoid personality domination","Forecast future market trends using expert opinion"],2,"Nominal Groups have members work in silence — each independently writes ideas. This maximises individual creativity and prevents dominant personalities from suppressing others' contributions.","Ebook","Other options: Interacting groups build team relationships through face-to-face debate · Delphi groups are specifically designed for forecasting using expert opinion · In Nominal groups, social interaction happens AFTER individual idea generation — not during"],
[5,"Break-Even Analysis in decision-making determines:",["The additional output obtained by adding one more unit of input","The level of sales at which Total Revenue equals Total Cost (Profit = 0)","The relationship between two financial variables in a statement","The optimum path through a set of alternative decisions and chance events"],1,"Break-Even Analysis identifies the level of sales at which Total Revenue exactly equals Total Cost — at this point, profit is zero. It helps managers assess minimum required sales.","Ebook","Other options: Additional output from one more unit of input describes Marginal Analysis (Paul Samuelson) · Relationship between two financial variables describes Ratio Analysis · Optimum path through decisions and chance events describes Decision Tree analysis"],
[5,"Linear Programming as an Operations Research technique is used for:",["Competitive strategy selection against rational opponents","Balancing service provided versus waiting time in queues","Optimal allocation of scarce resources with linear relationships","Building a computer model of a real system to test variables"],2,"Linear Programming finds the optimal allocation of limited (scarce) resources — it requires that all relationships between variables be linear in nature.","Ebook","Other options: Competitive strategy against opponents describes Gaming Theory · Balancing service vs waiting time describes Waiting-Line (Queuing) Theory · Building a computer model to test variables describes Simulation"],
[5,"Which of the following is an ADVANTAGE of group decision-making?",["Faster decision-making than individual managers","Clear individual accountability for outcomes","Less bias — collective contributions reduce individual bias","Avoids dominant members controlling the process"],2,"One advantage of group decision-making is that collective contributions from diverse members reduce individual bias — no single perspective dominates.","Ebook","Other options: Group DM is typically SLOWER, not faster — time is a key disadvantage · Individual accountability is REDUCED in group DM — a disadvantage, not an advantage · Dominant members CAN still overuse authority — this is listed as a disadvantage"],
[5,"Decision-making under CERTAINTY is most appropriate for:",["Major strategic decisions with long-term consequences","Launching a new product in an emerging market","Routine, repetitive day-to-day operational decisions","Investing in a new technology with unpredictable adoption rates"],2,"Certainty applies when all alternatives, conditions, and outcomes are known — this typically describes routine, repetitive operational decisions where outcomes are predictable.","Ebook","Other options: Strategic decisions with long-term consequences involve uncertainty — future consequences are unknown · New product launches in emerging markets involve risk (estimated probabilities) or uncertainty · Unpredictable adoption rates describe uncertainty — the highest level of informational challenge"],
[5,"The DSS (Decision Support System) differs from MIS in that DSS:",["Supports routine, structured decisions through standardised reports","Converts raw data into organised information for day-to-day management","Supports semi-structured and unstructured decisions by helping identify problems","Is computer-based only — MIS uses non-digital communication tools"],2,"DSS helps managers identify problems and finalise decisions — it supports semi-structured and unstructured decisions using communication technology and documents, unlike MIS which handles routine structured decisions.","Ebook","Other options: Routine structured decisions with standardised reports = MIS, not DSS · Converting raw data to organised information is the core function of MIS · Both MIS and DSS use computer-based tools — the distinction is in decision TYPE, not tools"],
[5,"Marginal Analysis in management decision-making was developed by:",["Herbert Simon","Paul Samuelson","Gerd Gigerenzer","Henry Fayol"],1,"Marginal Analysis — evaluating the additional (extra) output obtained from adding one more unit of input — is associated with economist Paul Samuelson.","Ebook","Other options: Herbert Simon developed the satisficer/maximiser concept and bounded rationality · Gerd Gigerenzer developed the simple heuristics theory · Henry Fayol developed the 14 principles of management — not a decision-making technique"],
[5,"Interacting Groups, as a type of group decision-making, are described as:",["The rarest form — used only for high-stakes strategic decisions","The most common form — members meet face-to-face to discuss, argue, and reach agreement","Used exclusively for forecasting — multiple questionnaire rounds without meetings","Always superior in quality to both Delphi and Nominal group methods"],1,"Interacting Groups are the most common form of group decision-making — face-to-face meetings where members discuss and debate. Political factors can influence outcomes and may limit creativity.","Ebook","Other options: Delphi groups use questionnaire rounds — they are NOT interacting groups · Interacting groups generate ideas but political dynamics can skew the outcome · Nominal groups (not interacting) are best for maximising individual creativity without dominance"],
[5,"Which limitation does NOT apply to the Rational Decision-Making Model?",["Humans cannot gather or process ALL information","Limited cognitive ability and time constraints","People often satisfice rather than optimise","It provides an ideal benchmark for evaluating actual decisions"],3,"Providing an ideal benchmark is actually a USE of the Rational Model — not a limitation. The limitations relate to the gap between the ideal rational process and real human cognitive constraints.","Ebook","Other options: All three other options are genuine limitations — humans cannot fully implement the rational model · The Rational Model is PRESCRIPTIVE — its utility as a benchmark is a strength, not a weakness · Risk-avoiding tendency (another limitation) can also disrupt the rational process"]
  ]
};
